  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual landscape diagram showing how climate change and human pressure interact to drive land degradation across a mountain-to-coast watershed. The scene links a headwater region with snow, glaciers, and thawing permafrost to forests, grassland, cropland, settlements, desert, floodplain, and coastline, emphasizing that changes upstream affect the whole system downstream.\n\nThe main message is that warming raises temperature, CO2, and aridity, while reducing snow cover and triggering permafrost thaw. These shifts alter ecosystem processes such as evapotranspiration, plant productivity, soil moisture, and soil organic carbon. At the same time, population growth increases demand for food, fuel, and housing, which appears to promote overgrazing and vegetation change. Bare soil and reduced vegetation cover then increase erosion.\n\nRed arrows mark key degradation pathways: grassland changing to shrubland, shrubland shifting toward desert, and overgrazing. A smaller inset highlights the grazing feedback: grazing lowers grassland, expands shrubland, increases soil compaction and vegetation patchiness, and leads to soil and wind erosion plus nutrient loss.\n\nOverall, the figure portrays degradation as a connected socio-ecological process involving climate, vegetation, soils, water flow, and human land use.",
